html(lang="en")
  include ../partials/_header.pug
  body
    include ../partials/_nav.pug

    .main
      .work-hero
        h1 TRAILS Design
          .hero-icon
            include ../img/icon--trails-logo.svg
        //-   .hero-tag TRAILS
        .work-hero-desc Design system leadership, engineering, and visual design for school mental health program
      
      p #[a.link(href='https://trailstowellness.org/' target='_blank') TRAILS] is a nonprofit organization that enables school staff to deliver mental health programming and education to students in K-12. I currently work as Design System Lead / UX Engineer as we prepare to launch a 2.0 version of our program.#[br]#[br]When I started with TRAILS, they had just received a new branding guide that focused mainly on marketing materials. It was my job to translate it into a product-compatible Design System that could be used for the web platform, where we host learning courses for school staff and resources they can use with students.

      //- pic of color ratio snippet
      figure.img-group
        img(src='/img/img--trails-1.png' alt='example of color ratios mocked up in two screens, with swatches below')
        figcaption.annotation Color ratios weren't specified by the brand book, so I adapted a vibe based on materials produced by the Content team. The goal was a generally warm tone and accessible tone.

      //- pic of color scale
      figure.img-group
        img(src='/img/img--trails-2.png' alt='each of the four main brand colors displayed in columns, showing each value step randing from 50 to 950')
        figcaption.annotation The existing brand book only specified a few shades of the main and secondary colors, which usually isn't enough for Product. I took the base hue and adapted a scale in the oklch format with lightness steps loosely based on Tailwind's default color scales. Because the brand uses both warm and cool tones, the neutrals are true neutral -- no chroma.
      
      //- pic of code snippet, globals.css
      figure.img-group
        img(src='/img/img--trails-3.png' alt='screenshot of globals.css code showing shadcn theme tokens and design tokens')
        figcaption.annotation Because of our small team, we decided to go with a Shadcn + Tailwind frontend setup. This made it fairly simple to set up Design System tokens and custom utilities and quickly establish a basic theme (although this came with its own set of limitations).

      //- pic of storybook docs
      figure.img-group
        img(src='/img/img--trails-4.png' alt='screenshot of Storybook documentation showing a bit of typography')
        figcaption.annotation And to help both designers and developers with guidelines and usage, I set up a few Storybook pages outside our component library to go over things like color scale and the pictured typography system. I wrote a few custom type classes in order to constrain our UI text to a collection of presets.

      p My being a design/dev hybrid let our designers work in low to mid-fidelity, and when the time came to build, I translated the functional intent from Figma wires while personally filling in the blanks like responsive behaviors and content constraints. This also meant that we didn't need to fully compose a Figma design library, as the number of designers on the team didn't quite justify the effort to maintain one, especially when there was so much plasticity from building out the 2.0 product.

      //- compilation of components
      figure.img-group
        img(src='/img/img--trails-5.png' alt='examples of components assembled in a loose grid')
        figcaption.annotation A snapshot of our component library

      p Another result of being a small team -- we leveraged Shadcn's headless component library as a base for commonly used primitives, while building upon them to develop a few custom components as necessary. Beyond just the basic theme variables though, I also adjusted the styling for each import so that felt like a unique brand and not just a barely customized Shadcn project that you see often on the web these days.#[br]#[br]I configured and built essentially all the components in our Storybook, including things like their skeleton loading states and their placement grids. Each component also had a basic set of Storybook stories to display and test their various states.

      //- pic of storybook entry
      figure.img-group
        img(src='/img/img--trails-6.png' alt='a storybook entry showing a list of items in different states')
        figcaption.annotation An example entry showing the different states of our module list item component

      p When the frontend build of 2.0 began, I configured the overall app shell (our app uses Tanstack Start), including the navigation, column system, and base responsive behavior. And as features started getting built out, and the PRs started rolling in, my role was the design sign off and review of every frontend PR. What I couldn't get to in a sprint (it's hard matching the pace of many other developers), we would merge in and create design pass tickets for the following sprint. It kind of worked... other than me feeling sometimes like I was drowning in PR review.#[br]#[br]In addition to heads-down dev work, another responsibility was injecting some fun and color into our content. The older version of the product used tiny pdf previews for resource cards, and basically no imagery elsewhere. I took on the task of creating a visual color-coding system based on our mental health skill categories, and began creating a set of thumbnails for the many item cards across the app.

      //- pic of thumbnails
      figure.img-group
        img(src='/img/img--trails-7.png' alt='a collection of illustrated thumbnail graphics')
        figcaption.annotation Our content can be categorized by subject matter, so that felt like a natural way to establish color coding and 'families' of resources. Less saturated icons were reserved for role-based content or miscellaneous categories.
      
      p In addition to all of the above, I also do lofi ideation work and partner with the (currently only other) designer to work through implementation nuances and iteration.#[br]#[br]We're currently in the thick of building and trimming of features for MVP, but below are a few screenshots of the actual current staging build.

      //- pic of staging home
      figure.img-group
        img(src='/img/img--trails-8.png' alt='screenshot of home page on staging, showing navigation and blocks of content')
        figcaption.annotation Home page with test seed data
      
      //- pic of staging pathway detail
      figure.img-group
        img(src='/img/img--trails-9.png' alt='screenshot of pathway detail page on staging, showing a course-like setup with accordion steps and line items within')
        figcaption.annotation Pathway page with (again) test seed data and placeholder thumbnails.
      
      .prev-next
        a.prev-button(href='index')
          span.prev-next-button__label PREV
          span.prev-next-button__text About
        a.next-button(href='verity')
          span.prev-next-button__label NEXT
          span.prev-next-button__text IBM Verity
          .prev-next-icon
            include ../img/icon--verity.svg